eorgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Go=
od morning, world. </span><span>Last week, we learned that Pope Leo XIV was=
 diagnosed with lung cancer. This week, we learned that surgery removed the=
 cancer entirely and he wouldn=E2=80=99t need further treatment.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span>Not so long ago, a lung cancer diagnosis would have been an automati=
c death sentence. It=E2=80=99s still deadly. But screening and a revolution=
 in cancer treatments have radically reduced death rates. That=E2=80=99s go=
od news, since rising life expectancies mean that many of us will get some =
type of cancer at some point in our lives. Worldwide, about one in five peo=
ple will get cancer. In some rich countries, it is more like one in two.</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>I had a fascinating conversation with my colleague Nina Agra=
wal, a health reporter who has been following these breakthroughs closely. =
We covered a lot of ground, including the tantalizing prospect of a cancer =
vaccine.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#3=
