<span>Humans vs. cancer</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Nina, t=
he pope is recovering from lung cancer surgery. Lung cancer is particularly=
 scary. But it=E2=80=99s a very different diagnosis than it would have been=
 in the past.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;col=
or:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Absolutely. There=E2=80=99s been a lot of pr=
ogress. The mortality rate for lung cancer has fallen and the five-year sur=
vival rate =E2=80=94 the portion of people who are still alive five years a=
fter being diagnosed =E2=80=94 has doubled in the United States, from about=
 15 percent in the late 1990s to around 30 percent now. It=E2=80=99s still =
not great compared with other types of cancer; lung cancer remains the lead=
ing cause of cancer death.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Wow. =
So what has changed in cancer treatment=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Advances vary b=
y cancer. In lung cancer, for example, a big one is targeted therapy. Many =
patients have a specific mutation =E2=80=94 often in a gene called EGFR =E2=
=80=94 and drugs that target that mutation have improved steadily over the =
last 20 years. That=E2=80=99s been a real game changer. Targeted therapy, a=
s the name implies, acts specifically on cancer cells and mostly leaves hea=
lthy cells alone, whereas traditional chemotherapy is toxic to most cells, =
including healthy ones.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia=
,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Breast cancer is another beneficia=
ry of targeted therapy. This is the most common cancer among women; about o=
ne in eight American women get it. But survival rates are now much higher. =
That=E2=80=99s because screening has gotten better, as have treatments: In =
addition to targeted therapies, there are also new immunotherapies that act=
ivate the patient=E2=80=99s immune system to attack the cancer. Those didn=
=E2=80=99t exist 15 years ago.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px =
georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">W=
hy are we seeing all these breakthroughs now=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:=
normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Two big th=
ings have happened. One is genetic sequencing. Doctors can sequence an indi=
vidual tumor, find its mutations and then tailor a treatment that specifica=
lly targets those mutations. For certain cancers, like some types of breast=
 cancer, it can also tell you whether a patient is likely to benefit from c=
hemotherapy, which can spare a lot of people that toxicity.</span></p><p st=
yle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><spa=
n>The other thing that=E2=80=99s really helped is understanding how the imm=
une system can be harnessed to fight cancer. Researchers have figured out h=
ow to better use our own immune system to attack cancer cells. They=E2=80=
=99ve developed drugs that essentially take the brakes off certain cells in=
 the immune system. Advanced melanoma, for example, used to be almost unive=
rsally deadly. It=E2=80=99s been totally transformed since this class of im=
munotherapies was first approved in 2011.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cel=
lpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=
