this week, a black avalanche of coal slurry tore down a hillside into Aberf=
an=E2=80=99s primary school, killing 116 children and 28 adults.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span>At the school assembly that day, they had sung =E2=80=9CAll Things B=
right and Beautiful=E2=80=9D and were about to start class when it happened=
=2E Most of the children who died were between 7 and 10 years old. (My son =
is 10. I hugged him a lot this week.)</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Aberfan is a shared =
national tragedy in Wales. It=E2=80=99s a term that conjures tears in grown=
 men two generations on.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>National tragedies involving the =
deaths of children resonate especially deeply. Like the 2008 Sichuan earthq=
uake in China, when more than 5,000 children died in collapsed schools. Or =
the Sewol ferry disaster, which claimed the lives of 250 school children in=
 South Korea in 2014. In the U.S., school shootings have become associated =
with names like Sandy Hook and Columbine.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>These are very d=
ifferent tragedies, but all of them involve a raw sense of futility and inj=
ustice. At the inquest in Aberfan, when a child=E2=80=99s cause of death wa=
s listed as asphyxia and multiple injuries, </span><a href=3D"https://nl.ny=
times.com/f/newsletter/qnaZUX27mXZ_E66fZ4g2sQ~~/AAAAARA~/Jw5hbWeVM8hWwYa3qn=
